68
00:04:53,840 --> 00:04:54,400
No, no, no, I'm not gonna do that.

69
00:04:54,720 --> 00:04:57,680
I think I remember I heard that it uh no longer in place.

70
00:04:57,680 --> 00:04:58,560
Yeah, it's a real shame.

71
00:04:58,560 --> 00:05:05,040
Uh uh to my knowledge i it uh kind of died out um about um some time around ninety nine.

72
00:05:05,040 --> 00:05:08,080
Are there any degreed programs in England now?

73
00:05:08,080 --> 00:05:08,960
I don't know of any.

74
00:05:08,960 --> 00:05:09,440
No.

75
00:05:09,440 --> 00:05:09,760
No.

76
00:05:09,760 --> 00:05:10,240
No.

77
00:05:10,480 --> 00:05:12,800
I'm not aware of any in the States here either.

78
00:05:12,800 --> 00:05:17,840
Yeah, I just think it was one of those uh it's it's gonna be down to econom economics more than anything.

79
00:05:17,840 --> 00:05:24,960
Uh I guess, you know, that it's gonna be a niche market, there's not that many people within the art world uh who are interested in using holography.

80
00:05:25,280 --> 00:05:26,400
Why is that?

81
00:05:26,400 --> 00:05:28,240
Uh, you know, that's a really good question.

82
00:05:28,240 --> 00:05:34,360
I I think it's one of those complex things we could we can break down into kind of public awareness.

83
00:05:34,360 --> 00:05:43,320
Um for my short time in in the sphere of holography it seems to be one of those things that um goes through kind of peaks and troughs in terms of its trend.

84
00:05:43,320 --> 00:05:56,680
Um you know it's kind of really popularized um maybe by I don't know sci fi or something like that or by a big exhibition being put out by by a company and sort of the public awareness of it raises it a little while and people get excited about it.

85
00:05:56,680 --> 00:05:58,520
So I'm not sure if I find that as well, yeah.

86
00:05:58,520 --> 00:05:58,920
Yeah.

87
00:05:58,920 --> 00:06:11,080
And then and then that sort of seems to peter out again 'cause people you know think I I ultimately I guess it it's still for many people's minds constrained to that of gimmick um rather than being taken seriously.

88
00:06:11,080 --> 00:06:23,640
Certainly that's the response that um we found within the sort of main artistic community the kind of fine artists kind of think it's because it's so technical there's so many uh chemicals involved and optical equipment and it's pretty difficult.

89
00:06:23,640 --> 00:06:26,840
Um that does put a constraint on creativity.

90
00:06:26,840 --> 00:06:30,600
It's kinda hard to find workarounds to to be creative with it.

91
00:06:30,600 --> 00:06:36,840
Um which is obviously but yet there's so many beautiful artistic holograms have been created over the years.

92
00:06:36,840 --> 00:06:41,400
Yeah well I think that really is the skill of of those people involved.

93
00:06:41,400 --> 00:06:50,280
I think to get really creative um and and produce uh in a a work of art which will stand alongside a kind of conventional work of art I think is really quite an achievement.

94
00:06:50,280 --> 00:07:00,640
Um now some of your work you've worked with pulse holography as well I know I've seen a few of your hologram is that you that's in the pulse holograms that I've seen I think the Jonathan Ross collection.

95
00:07:01,080 --> 00:07:01,600
Yes it is.

96
00:07:01,600 --> 00:07:02,120
Yeah.

97
00:07:02,120 --> 00:07:19,040
Um just the simple expedient being that uh it's cheaper um uh you know, attending the Royal College of Art was a real dream come true, it was really fantastic, but um the bottom line is uh you start being involved in holography and I'm sure many of your listeners be aware it gets pretty expensive part of the time.

98
00:07:14,440 --> 00:07:23,680
Um you know the holographic films and the chemicals involved uh don't come cheap.

99
00:07:23,680 --> 00:07:36,880
Uh and then I certainly didn't have time and and money to spend on bringing in uh external models, you know, using professional people um that way so it's easiest just to kind of jump to the side of the plate and press the button with myself as a subject matter.

100
00:07:36,880 --> 00:07:37,600
Yeah.

101
