00:09:59,960 --> 00:10:00,520
Uh-huh.

139
00:10:00,520 --> 00:10:01,720
Okay, sure.

140
00:10:01,720 --> 00:10:06,920
Um I think from memory that was one of the series towards the end of my time at the Royal College of Art.

141
00:10:06,920 --> 00:10:13,640
Uh and that was just really starting to experiment um with that technique of of um reduction holography.

142
00:10:13,640 --> 00:10:17,720
Yeah, that's the only piece I've ha I have of a pulsed reduction.

143
00:10:17,720 --> 00:10:18,200
Uh huh.

144
00:10:18,200 --> 00:10:21,320
And only the second one that I've ever seen in my life.

145
00:10:21,320 --> 00:10:22,040
Oh, okay.

146
00:10:22,520 --> 00:10:27,640
Well, um, you know, there are quite a lot of uh accomplished artists out there using it very successfully.

147
00:10:27,640 --> 00:10:37,640
Uh it's not too difficult technique once you've created a master hologram, in this case uh a pulsed laser, so you know, you actually got the opportunity to record uh a a living person.

148
00:10:37,640 --> 00:10:39,960
Um which is great experience.

149
00:10:39,960 --> 00:10:54,000
Then just simply in the transfer process, uh putting uh a large um uh uh uh convex lens, big glass lens between the master plate and the transfer plate allows it uh this optical reduction to take place.

150
00:10:54,000 --> 00:10:58,480
There are a number of of workarounds that that have to be found to to make it actually happen.

151
00:10:58,880 --> 00:11:09,920
Um but um it's certainly an interesting process and and is equivalent then to you know kind of photographic negative and that you can make the the final r resulting image less than life size.

152
00:11:09,920 --> 00:11:12,880
Um which is cheaper for for one thing.

