00:13:55,080 --> 00:13:55,560
Great.

193
00:13:55,560 --> 00:13:58,600
What was your biggest frustration with holography?

194
00:13:58,600 --> 00:14:05,880
Can you think of what it was that just drove you nuts and you just couldn't get past it in the creative process working with it.

195
00:14:05,880 --> 00:14:20,640
Um I think uh the putting the quite large financial issues aside because um you know to to uh go back to the subject we were just talking about with emotions, there were so few back then and and even less now, so few producers of this very specialized emulsion.

196
00:14:20,640 --> 00:14:25,600
Um and and it is such a specialized process that um by its nature it was very expensive material.

197
00:14:25,600 --> 00:14:35,520
Certainly when you start to get up into larger, you know, the sort of thirty by forty, fifty by sixty in case of film uh sizes, it really becomes quite prohibitive for many people.

198
00:14:35,520 --> 00:14:40,480
But that that aside, I think it was just uh the lack of colour.

199
00:14:40,480 --> 00:14:58,160
That's why I really got interested in those those latter pieces, uh that trip dick who talked about one of the pieces, Icarus uh and Solace, um, were really all about uh trying to get uh the pseudo colour technique so actually getting some semblance of of more natural colour happening in there.

200
00:14:58,160 --> 00:15:05,120
And I think that's one of the things for me holography by its nature it's de facto process uh gives a very, very pure light.

201
00:15:05,120 --> 00:15:10,160
Uh it can be very bright colours, kind of uh prismatic rainbow colours, which is very appealing.

202
00:15:10,160 --> 00:15:19,120
Um but I think the fact that uh for example in a in a tick typical uh hologram portrait, it's you know, uh monocolor, you know, it's green or yellow.

203
00:15:19,120 --> 00:15:19,600
Yes.

204
00:15:19,600 --> 00:15:22,960
Um and obviously, you know, uh that's not true to life.

